{"step_index":702,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T21:56:13Z","thinking":"**Defining Pattern Naming**\n\nI'm currently focused on defining the naming conventions for interview patterns. Thinking about how to clearly categorize questions like \"Two Sum\" under both \"Hash Map & Complement Lookup\" and \"Two Pointers\" is tricky. I want names that are both accurate and easy to understand. I'm also considering how to best represent categories.\n\n\n**Categorizing Interview Patterns**\n\nI've been working on categorizing various interview questions. I've now categorized questions like \"Contains Duplicate\" as `\"Hash Set, Frequency Map, Sorting\"`, and \"Valid Anagram\" as `\"Frequency Array, Hash Table, Sorting\"`. I also grouped questions like \"Group Anagrams\" as `\"Categorize by Sorted Key, Frequency Tuple Hashing\"`. I'm also planning to represent \"Top K Frequent Elements\" with `\"Bucket Sort, Min-Heap, Quickselect\"`, and so on for several more questions. I want to create precise labels.\n\n\n**Expanding Pattern Nomenclature**\n\nI've been expanding the pattern nomenclature. I've categorized \"Coin Change\" under `\"Dynamic Programming (Unbounded Knapsack), BFS Shortest Path\"`, and \"Maximum Subarray\" with `\"Kadane's Algorithm, Dynamic Programming (1D DP)\"`. \"Invert Binary Tree\" goes with `\"Tree DFS / Recursion, BFS Level-Order Queue\"`. I'm using very precise, specific descriptions, e.g. \"Valid Parentheses\" as `\"Stack, Matching Pair Hash Map\"`. I am also working on dynamic high-yield pattern variations.\n\n\n**Analyzing Interview Strategies**\n\nI'm now thinking about how to recognize these patterns during an interview and the key observations that trigger them. My process is to understand brute force approaches and why they fail, followed by intermediate methods with space-time tradeoffs, and finally, interview-ready optimal solutions. I'm also planning to incorporate a section about common pitfal